class:: PMWave
summary:: Physical modelling plate
related:: Classes/Pluck
categories:: UGens>PhysicalModels

description::

A 4x4 toroidal digital waveguide mesh of 64 delay lines (both directions of 32 waveguides). Every delay line has
its own length, glide time and wander, and glides towards its target, so the plate can be stretched in real time. Rails are read with cubic
interpolation, and losses are applied per rail and scaled with the rail length, so the decay times stay the same
whatever the plate size.

Rail lengths range from 0.1 ms to 40 ms. The rail buffers come from the server's real-time memory: about 0.5 MB
per instance at 44.1/48 kHz and 1 MB at 88.2/96 kHz. Raise code::ServerOptions.memSize:: for many instances; if
the allocation fails the UGen posts an error and outputs silence.

The excitation enters at junction (0,0) without damping it; two channels are picked up at (1,2) and (0,2).


classmethods::

method::ar

argument::in
Excitation, injected at junction (0,0).

argument::decay
Low-frequency decay time (T60) in seconds. Read once per control block.

argument::time
Plate size, 0..1, on an exponential scale: 0 gives 40 ms rails (a large, dense, room-like plate), 1 gives 0.1 ms
rails. Equal steps are equal pitch intervals (about 0.86 octave per 0.1).

argument::glide
How fast the plate follows a new code::time::, in seconds (see code::glideMode::). Read once per control block.
However short, a rail never changes by more than one sample per sample.

argument::hfDecay
High-frequency decay time (T60 at Nyquist) in seconds. Read once per control block.

argument::rand1
Spread of the 64 delay line lengths (each with its own random value), as a ratio: each sits within ×/÷ (1 + rand1) of the size set by
code::time:: (0 = regular mesh, a pitched comb; 0.15 and above = irregular, diffuse).

argument::rand2
Spread of the delay lines' glide times, as a ratio: ×/÷ (1 + rand2), so the plate deforms while it is stretched.
Read once per control block.

argument::wander
Depth of a slow, independent random modulation (0.1-0.4 Hz) of every delay line length, as a ratio: ×/÷ (1 + wander).
Small values (0.002-0.02) keep the modes moving like a modulated reverb; larger values become audible chorus.

argument::glideMode
0: lag. code::glide:: is the time to arrive (60 dB in log length, like link::Classes/Lag::) whatever the distance;
the pitch bend of what is ringing is strongest at the start of the glide.
1: constant rate. Every delay line moves at a steady speed in log length, code::glide:: seconds per octave, so
larger stretches take longer and the bend stays steady, even in pitch. Read once per control block.


examples::

code::

{ PMWave.ar(Impulse.ar(0.5) * 0.5, decay: 4, time: 0.4, hfDecay: 0.8) }.play

// large and diffuse
{ PMWave.ar(Impulse.ar(0.25) * 0.5, decay: 6, time: 0.05, hfDecay: 0.6, rand1: 0.25, wander: 0.01) }.play

// stretch the plate while it rings
{ PMWave.ar(Impulse.ar(0.25) * 0.5, 6, LFNoise1.ar(0.2).range(0.1, 0.7), 1.5, 1.5, 0.2, 0.5) }.play

::
